ge too, so that fetch is blocked. When the fetch fails, the app shows \"NOT FOUND – No note lives at this address\" but still returns HTTP 200, and Google treats that as a Soft 404. The URL Inspection export shows exactly that text as the rendered page. Browsers and curl don't obey `robots.txt`, so the pages look fine to you. The dates line up: the two notes last crawled in late August are still indexed, and every note crawled after Sep 7 is a Soft 404.\n\n**What I changed:**\n- **`site/robots.txt`:** added `Allow: /v1/notes` so the public read endpoints can be fetched again. I also added `Disallow: /v1/notes/*/reactions` and kept `Disallow: /v1/` and `/admin/`. Google uses the longest matching rule, so reactions, `/v1/me`, drafts and auth stay blocked. I checked this with a small script that applies the rules the way Google does: the public read paths are allowed and everything else is blocked.\n- **`site/app.js`:** only a real 404 from the API now shows \"NOT FOUND\", and it also sets the page to `noindex`. Any other failure (blocked, network, 5xx) shows a neutral \"Couldn't load this page\" message, so a temporary problem no longer looks like a missing page.\n\nNothing has been deployed or submitted. Once you deploy, ask Search Console to recrawl `robots.txt`, then run a live test on `/n/drip-line-pressure` and request indexing for the four Soft 404 URLs. Those steps are in `ANSWER.md`.","ttft_ms":4099,"type":"result","duration_ms":51918,"uuid":"6a2e1406-61b9-4913-ba42-fc066d4e23e7","ttft_stream_ms":2736,"time_to_request_ms":46,"first_content_frame_ms":2737,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
